course: dictionary
lesson: 8413
title: Water words in the countryside
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A runnel is a very small stream, or a little channel of running water. Think of rain trickling down the side of a path or along a gutter after a shower.
explain: Runnel is a noun, so you can have one runnel or many runnels. Writers use it to paint a quiet, detailed picture of the countryside, the tiny water-ways you only notice when you look closely.
explain: «They search the hedges, the hollow trees, the runnels and their little culverts.» The words around runnel are the clues. Hedges and hollow trees are places in the countryside, and runnels sit in that list as small places where water runs. Culverts are little tunnels for water, so runnels are small and watery too.
fact: Runnel means a very small stream or brook.
fact: Runnel can also mean a small channel of running water, such as rain running down a path or gutter.
fact: «Runnels» is the plural of runnel.
fact: Runnels are smaller than streams.
fact: «Culverts» means little tunnels that carry water under a path.
exercise: What is a runnel? | *A very small stream or brook | A large lake with a boat on it | A thick hedge along a road | A tall hill covered in trees
exercise: «They search the hedges, the hollow trees, the runnels and their little culverts.» Which word means little tunnels that carry water under a path? | runnels | *culverts | hollow | hedges
exercise: After the rain, water trickles down the gutter in a thin line. Which word fits best? | *runnel | ancient | hedge | hollow
test: What is the plural of runnel? | runnellers | *runnels | runnel
test: Which is smaller: a stream or a runnel? | A river | *A runnel | They are both the same size | A stream
exam: Which sentence uses runnel correctly? | She ate a runnel for lunch. | *After the rain, a runnel of water ran down the path. | The runnel was a huge ocean liner.
